WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.449
Back in the restaurant, a long night is over.

2
00:00:05.449 --> 00:00:08.137
A customer says the soup took forty minutes.

3
00:00:08.137 --> 00:00:10.336
The cook remembers making it in six.

4
00:00:10.567 --> 00:00:14.368
And here's the awkward part: both of them are telling the truth.

5
00:00:14.368 --> 00:00:19.476
The bowl may have waited for the only waiter, or for the kitchen to finish washing up.

6
00:00:19.476 --> 00:00:23.515
Two honest people, one bowl of soup, and nobody wrote anything down.

7
00:00:24.433 --> 00:00:26.716
Our program has the same argument.

8
00:00:26.716 --> 00:00:34.101
The report service fetches each document from a document store: another program that answers over the network.

9
00:00:34.101 --> 00:00:39.941
Some are slow, so we wrap a timer around the call, and log: fetch took 46 milliseconds.

10
00:00:40.167 --> 00:00:43.397
So here's the question that's easy to be shy about.

11
00:00:43.397 --> 00:00:45.804
The log line already has the duration.

12
00:00:45.804 --> 00:00:48.464
What more could any tool possibly tell us?

13
00:00:51.633 --> 00:00:53.762
Look at what that line can't say.

14
00:00:53.762 --> 00:00:57.698
Was every thread paused right then, for a garbage collection?

15
00:00:57.698 --> 00:01:01.246
The garbage collector, or GC, frees memory nobody uses.

16
00:01:01.246 --> 00:01:06.795
Did our thread wait for a lock, a guard that lets one thread at a time into some code?

17
00:01:06.795 --> 00:01:09.117
Did something grab a pile of memory?

18
00:01:09.333 --> 00:01:13.488
It's a witness who watched one table all night, on a clock of its own.

19
00:01:13.488 --> 00:01:15.981
Honest, and blind to the rest of the room.

20
00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:20.962
What we'd love is one log of everything the kitchen did, on one clock.

21
00:01:20.962 --> 00:01:25.895
And cheap enough to keep every night, because nobody knows which night will go wrong.

22
00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:32.123
The JVM, the program that runs your Java and Kotlin, keeps a log like that.

23
00:01:32.123 --> 00:01:36.460
It's already in your JDK: JDK Flight Recorder, or JFR.

24
00:01:37.367 --> 00:01:39.317
Let's map the kitchen onto it.

25
00:01:39.317 --> 00:01:42.762
Each cook is a thread, a line of work in the program.

26
00:01:42.762 --> 00:01:53.877
Each line in the log book is an event: a small record with a start time, often a duration, the thread, and usually a stack trace, the chain of method calls that led there.

27
00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:59.058
The whole kitchen washing up is a GC pause, and it hits every thread at once.

28
00:01:59.058 --> 00:02:02.213
Waiting for the only waiter is a wait for a lock.

29
00:02:02.213 --> 00:02:08.008
And the JVM writes plenty more on its own, like samples of what running threads are doing.

30
00:02:08.233 --> 00:02:14.070
Our own code can add lines too, like the cook's own notes: one for every call to the store.

31
00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:16.186
And all of them sit on one clock.

32
00:02:16.186 --> 00:02:17.661
That's the whole point.

33
00:02:21.767 --> 00:02:24.114
Here the kitchen picture has a limit.

34
00:02:24.114 --> 00:02:26.144
The log covers our kitchen only.

35
00:02:26.144 --> 00:02:30.077
The document store is like the farm that grows the vegetables.

36
00:02:30.077 --> 00:02:35.660
If the farm is slow, our log shows us waiting, but not why: the farm keeps its own book.

37
00:02:36.567 --> 00:02:39.740
One session of this log is called a recording.

38
00:02:39.740 --> 00:02:43.878
It keeps events in memory, and moves them to disk in chunks.

39
00:02:43.878 --> 00:02:48.431
Picture a log book that tears out its oldest pages when it's full.

40
00:02:48.431 --> 00:02:50.707
maxage says how far back to keep.

41
00:02:50.707 --> 00:02:52.639
maxsize says how many bytes.

42
00:02:52.639 --> 00:02:55.743
Set neither, and the JVM keeps 250 megabytes.

43
00:02:56.667 --> 00:03:01.279
You can start a recording with one option on the java command.

44
00:03:01.279 --> 00:03:08.346
Or on a JVM that's already running, with jcmd, a JDK tool that sends commands to a running JVM.

45
00:03:08.346 --> 00:03:11.321
Here's what the report service answered.

46
00:03:11.533 --> 00:03:17.275
When something goes wrong, JFR.dump copies the pages so far into a file, and stops nothing.

47
00:03:17.275 --> 00:03:21.061
You don't have to close the restaurant to read the log book.

48
00:03:21.061 --> 00:03:25.036
When the bad night comes, the last hour is already in the book.

49
00:03:25.967 --> 00:03:28.604
But always on only works if it's cheap.

50
00:03:28.604 --> 00:03:30.903
JFR comes with two settings files.

51
00:03:30.903 --> 00:03:40.777
default calls itself safe for continuous use in production, typically under 1 percent overhead, and samples running threads every 20 milliseconds.

52
00:03:40.777 --> 00:03:44.293
profile says around 2 percent, and samples every 10.

53
00:03:44.293 --> 00:03:47.675
So: default to leave on, profile for a short hunt.

54
00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:51.309
Now let's ask a real recording what's in it.

55
00:03:51.309 --> 00:03:55.311
The lesson's demo runs four threads for 4 seconds, under profile.

56
00:03:55.311 --> 00:04:00.668
Each counts words, asks a fake store for a document, and sometimes holds a shared lock.

57
00:04:00.668 --> 00:04:03.131
Which event will have the highest count?

58
00:04:03.131 --> 00:04:06.148
And how many samples of running code will we get?

59
00:04:09.933 --> 00:04:14.866
jfr summary lists every event type, with a count.

60
00:04:14.866 --> 00:04:20.906
At the top, the garbage collector's paperwork: 1,951 events.

61
00:04:20.906 --> 00:04:23.825
Then ours: 1,361 store calls.

62
00:04:23.825 --> 00:04:28.456
Then 1,207 allocation samples, and 560 sleeps.

63
00:04:28.667 --> 00:04:31.859
Only the garbage collector writes more than we do.

64
00:04:31.859 --> 00:04:34.541
Every kitchen has one colleague like that.

65
00:04:34.541 --> 00:04:38.116
And its lines sit right next to ours, on the same clock.

66
00:04:38.333 --> 00:04:40.081
Now look near the bottom.

67
00:04:40.081 --> 00:04:46.304
Execution samples, the snapshots of running code: just 40, in 4 seconds, on four threads.

68
00:04:46.533 --> 00:04:47.248
Why so few?

69
00:04:47.248 --> 00:04:55.178
The threads spent most of their time asleep, waiting for the store, and the sampler only counts threads running Java code.

70
00:04:55.178 --> 00:04:58.038
A cook waiting for a delivery isn't cooking.

71
00:04:58.038 --> 00:04:59.403
Keep that 40 in mind.

72
00:05:00.333 --> 00:05:01.402
So, a few rules.

73
00:05:01.402 --> 00:05:02.871
Set maxage or maxsize.

74
00:05:02.871 --> 00:05:06.210
Leave default on, and use profile for short hunts.

75
00:05:06.210 --> 00:05:09.282
Don't expect it to see inside another program.

76
00:05:09.282 --> 00:05:15.960
And 40 samples can spot a method that takes most of the time, but can't show what happens inside it.

77
00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:19.232
That takes thousands of samples: the next lesson.

78
00:05:20.133 --> 00:05:23.660
So, what can a recording tell you that the log line can't?

79
00:05:23.660 --> 00:05:26.821
What else happened at the same moment, on one clock.

80
00:05:26.821 --> 00:05:30.713
A cheap recording that's always on is evidence you already have.

81
00:05:30.713 --> 00:05:32.597
Dump it, instead of restarting.

82
00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:33.720
Quick check.

83
00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:36.633
A request was slow at 9:14 last night.

84
00:05:36.633 --> 00:05:42.000
The program has run a recording all week, with maxage set to one hour.

85
00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:43.149
It's now 10:30.

86
00:05:43.149 --> 00:05:45.143
Is 9:14 still in the book?

87
00:05:48.333 --> 00:05:48.546
No.

88
00:05:48.546 --> 00:05:53.157
The book keeps only the last hour, so 9:14 was torn out at 10:14.

89
00:05:53.157 --> 00:05:57.271
Keep enough book to cover the time it takes you to notice.
